EX={
"turning-65-with-a-401k-medicare-and-retirement-savings":"""## What to bring to a planning conversation
To make a short conversation productive, gather your most recent 401(k) statement, a rough monthly budget, your expected Social Security start date, a list of doctors and medications, and any employer benefit documents. With those in hand, it is much easier to line up Medicare timing, premium effects, and retirement income choices in the right order instead of making them one at a time.
""",
"what-is-indexed-universal-life-iul-how-it-works-cost-risks":"""## Compare it with simpler alternatives
Before committing, ask for a side-by-side with a simpler approach: a term policy for the years you need protection, plus separate savings in tax-advantaged accounts. Sometimes the simple route wins on cost, and sometimes permanent coverage fits better because of estate or business needs. Seeing both on paper, with the same assumptions, is the best way to judge whether the added complexity of an IUL is earning its keep.
""",
"how-much-does-health-insurance-cost-without-a-job":"""## An example of how subsidies change the picture
Consider two households that both buy the same Silver plan with the same list price. One has a modest household income and qualifies for a substantial premium tax credit, so its monthly bill is small. The other has a higher income and receives little or no credit, so it pays much closer to the list price. The plans are identical, but the real cost is not, which is why a quote without your income and household details is only a rough guess.

## What to do first
Make a short list: your expected household income, who needs coverage, your doctors, and your medications. With those four items, a licensed agent can produce a realistic comparison in minutes, including whether COBRA, a Marketplace plan, or Medicaid is likely to fit your budget best.
""",
"aca-open-enrollment-deadline-can-i-sign-up-late":"""## A simple checklist for enrollment season
Before the window opens, confirm your dates, update your household income estimate, and make a list of doctors and prescriptions. When it opens, compare at least three plans, enroll well before the last day, pay the first premium, and save your confirmation. After enrolling, check that your insurer has your correct address and that your ID card arrives. Following these steps avoids the most common problems we see: missed deadlines, unpaid first premiums, and plans that do not include the doctors people expected.

## Employer plans have their own windows
If you get coverage through a job, your employer sets its own open enrollment period, usually once a year. Missing that window can also leave you waiting for a qualifying life event, so check your benefits calendar as well.
""",
"can-i-keep-my-aca-subsidy-if-my-income-changes":"""## Keep good records
Whatever happens to your income, keep copies of pay stubs, invoices, unemployment statements, and the Marketplace notices you receive. At tax time, you will receive a form from the Marketplace showing the advance credits paid on your behalf, and your tax preparer will need it. Having your income records organized makes reconciliation simpler and helps you catch mistakes before they cost you.

## When to call before changing anything
If you are thinking of taking on a big contract, quitting a job, or selling an asset, a quick conversation first can help you see how it might affect your subsidy. It is easier to plan around a change than to unwind it after the fact.
""",
"deductible-vs-out-of-pocket-maximum-explained":"""## Putting it together
When you shop, list three numbers for every plan: the premium you pay each month, the deductible you pay before sharing begins, and the out-of-pocket maximum that caps your worst year. Then multiply the premium by twelve and add the maximum to see the most the plan could cost you in a bad year, and add only the premium to see the best-case cost. That range, not just the monthly price, tells you which plan truly fits your budget and your appetite for risk.

## Special cases to know about
Some services, such as certain preventive visits, may be covered before you meet your deductible. Some plans also apply copays for primary care visits and generic drugs even if you have not met the deductible. Read the summary of benefits to see which services follow which rule, because those details change what a plan really costs you in everyday use.
""",
"hmo-vs-ppo-vs-epo-which-health-plan-type-is-right":"""## A quick scenario
A family with young children and a trusted pediatrician may prefer whichever plan type includes that practice and the nearby children's hospital, even if the premium is a little higher. A couple who rarely uses care and who is happy with a local network might choose an HMO and save on premiums. A person with a long-standing specialist outside the main network may pay more for a PPO to keep that relationship. There is no universal winner, only the best match to your habits.

## Check the network every year
Networks can change from one plan year to the next. Even if you are staying with the same plan type, confirm your doctors again each fall before you renew, and ask whether the plan is changing hospitals or medical groups.
""",
}
EX["aca-open-enrollment-deadline-can-i-sign-up-late"]+="""
## If you are unsure whether your event qualifies
Rules for qualifying events have specific definitions and documentation requirements. If you are not sure whether your situation counts, ask before assuming you are out of luck. A short call can confirm whether you have a window, how long it lasts, and which documents you will need to submit.
"""
EX["can-i-keep-my-aca-subsidy-if-my-income-changes"]+="""
## Questions worth asking your tax preparer
Ask how advance credits will be reconciled on your return, whether any repayment limits apply under current law, and how self-employment deductions affect the income figure used for the credit. Bringing these questions early, not in April, gives you time to adjust your estimate and your monthly credit before the year ends.
"""
EX["deductible-vs-out-of-pocket-maximum-explained"]+="""
## Do not ignore network rules
Remember that the out-of-pocket maximum usually applies only to in-network care. Out-of-network services may have a separate, higher limit or no limit at all, which is why confirming that your providers are in network matters as much as the numbers on the plan summary.
"""
EX["hmo-vs-ppo-vs-epo-which-health-plan-type-is-right"]+="""
## Prescription coverage is separate from plan type
Plan type does not tell you how drugs are covered. Two PPOs can have very different drug lists and pharmacy networks, so check your medications for each plan individually rather than assuming they behave alike.
"""
